> The problem is that in the dataset I'm working with I have 189 respondents and 40 items, therefore there are more estimated parameters (160) for two dimensions than response patterns (153). To assure the analyses, according to Reckase, the parameter estimates should be smaller than the response patterns.

I would say it should be much smaller. For most ML estimation its recommended to have at 5 times the sample size per parameter estimated. Of course that's a general recommendation since it depends on the type of parameters estimated. For instance, in Rasch models you can have a substantially smaller sample size since all that is being estimated are intercepts, which are extremely stable to begin with.

>
>
>
> I would like to reduce the parameters by fixing only a few hits at random (guess) and thus ensure the reduction guess of the estimated parameters, since I already have a few hits at zero estimated randomly.

Change the itemtype's you want to '2PL' instead, and supply a vector of guess values for the 'guess =' input. Both itemtype and guess should be vectors then with the lengths equal to the number of items. There is an early example of this in the ?mirt documentation with the LSAT7 dataset.

Phil

